{"title":"Circle Theorems","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>Below is a brief introduction to the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> most fundamental circle theorems.\n</p><p>Theorem <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />: If a line is drawn from the center of a circle to the midpoint of a chord, the lines are perpendicular. Vice versa, if a line is drawn from the center of a circle and is perpendicular to the chord, the point of the intersection is the midpoint of the chord.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/7/1d76edd772593d4c4bce39b6c9aca0af2fdbff73.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy]draw(circle((0,0),5)); draw((0,0)--(0,5)); draw((-3,4)--(3,4)); draw(rightanglemark((0,0),(0,4),(3,4))); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"252\" />\n</p><p>Theorem <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />: If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> radii are drawn from the center of the circle and the points where the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> radii lines intersect the circle meet at another point, the interior degree measure created by the radii is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> times the measure of the interior degree measure of the angle created by the chords. This theorem is rather visual, so refer to the pictures below. Remember the shapes the lines create must be an arrow head or a double triangle, as shown in pictures <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, respectively.\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p>Arrow Head\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/2/4d2165a80e2df707aba5d3c71417ef2f43400f8e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy]draw(circle((0,0),5)); draw((-3.15,-3.883)--(-0.99, 4.901)); draw((2.59, -4.277)--(-0.99, 4.901)); draw((-3.15, -3.883)--(0,0)); draw((2.59, -4.277)--(0,0)); dot((-3.15,-3.883)); label(&quot;A&quot;,(-3.15,-3.883),S); dot((2.59,-4.277)); label(&quot;B&quot;,(2.59,-4.277),S); dot((-0.99,4.901)); label(&quot;C&quot;,(-0.99,4.90 1),N); dot((0,0)); label(&quot;D&quot;,(0,0),N); markscalefactor=0.08; draw(anglemark((-3.15,-3.883),(0,0),(2.59,-4.277))); label(&quot;${2x}^\\circ$&quot;,anglemark((-3.15,-3.883),(0,0),(2.59,-4.277)),S,green); draw(anglemark((-3.15,-3.883),(-0.99,4.901),(2.59,-4.277))); label(&quot;${x}^\\circ$&quot;,anglemark((-3.15,-3.883),(-0.99,4.901),(2.59,-4.277)),SE,green); [/asy]\" width=\"228\" height=\"252\" />\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p>Double Triangle\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/1/d21497ebd6404d973c67bbc2f9727b58b3d52030.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy]draw(circle((0,0),5)); draw((-3.15,-3.883)--(4.913,-0.929)); draw((2.59, -4.277)--(4.913,-0.929)); draw((-3.15, -3.883)--(0,0)); draw((2.59, -4.277)--(0,0)); dot((-3.15,-3.883)); label(&quot;A&quot;,(-3.15,-3.883),S); dot((2.59,-4.277)); label(&quot;B&quot;,(2.59,-4.277),S); dot((4.913,-0.929)); label(&quot;C&quot;,(4.913,-0.929),N); dot((0,0)); label(&quot;D&quot;,(0,0),N); markscalefactor=0.08; draw(anglemark((-3.15,-3.883),(0,0),(2.59,-4.277))); label(&quot;${2x}^\\circ$&quot;,anglemark((-3.15,-3.883),(0,0),(2.59,-4.277)),S,green); draw(anglemark((-3.15,-3.883),(4.913,-0.929),(2.59,-4.277))); label(&quot;${x}^\\circ$&quot;,anglemark((-3.15,-3.883),(4.913,-0.929),(2.59,-4.277)),SW,green); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"248\" />\n</p><p><br />\nTheorem <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />: If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> radii form a straight line or a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/4/c4460b5100c3ce982ab5ebb20a8c3871e9cfdc54.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$180^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" /> angle, this line is the diameter.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/f/e2f5da52a70ccf85bfa5b3e7fb1376b3c3e2deb0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy]draw(circle((0,0),5)); draw((-5,0)--(0,0),green); draw((0,0)--(5,0),red); dot((-5,0)); label(&quot;A&quot;,(-5,0),W); dot((0,0)); label(&quot;B&quot;,(0,0),N); dot((5,0)); label(&quot;C&quot;,(5,0),E); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"208\" />\nAs seen in the picture above, radii <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> form at straight line or a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/4/c4460b5100c3ce982ab5ebb20a8c3871e9cfdc54.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$180^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" /> angle. Therefore, segment <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/7/a179ff2638e4799cadd820db205c2beff6299ce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> is a diameter of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/8/0f84f9934b119c184a22012450f8056373e35ee4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\bigodot{B}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -8px\" width=\"44\" height=\"25\" />.\n</p><p>Theorem <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />:The opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral sum to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/4/c4460b5100c3ce982ab5ebb20a8c3871e9cfdc54.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$180^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/d/39d9a4782a00d5ca07dcbb4efa320f4956f34c24.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy]draw(circle((0,0),5)); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"252\" />\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018024638\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.010 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.100 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 70/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 5631/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:17498:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018024638 and revision id 105537. Rendering 